### 4. `fetchHistory` on logout fetches without `await`

**Severity**: Low — Potential race condition  
**File**: [auth-context.tsx](file:///c:/Users/DELL/Documents/personal%20proejcts/image%20gen/iprintr/src/app/context/auth-context.tsx#L87-L90)

```typescript
const logout = () => {
  setUser(null);
  fetchHistory(null); // ← not awaited, fire-and-forget
};
```

Since `logout` isn't `async`, `fetchHistory(null)` fires without being awaited. If it fails, the error is silently swallowed. More importantly, the `useEffect` on `user` will *also* call `fetchHistory(null)` when `setUser(null)` triggers a re-render — so you get **two concurrent fetches** for the same data.

---

### 5. Double history fetch on login/signup

**Severity**: Low — Wasted network call  
**File**: [auth-context.tsx](file:///c:/Users/DELL/Documents/personal%20proejcts/image%20gen/iprintr/src/app/context/auth-context.tsx#L55-L69)

```typescript
const login = async (email: string, name: string) => {
  // ...
  setUser(data.user);          // triggers useEffect → fetchHistory(data.user.id)
  await fetchHistory(data.user.id); // also fetches history explicitly
};
```

`setUser` triggers the `useEffect` that calls `fetchHistory(user?.id)`, **and** the next line also calls `fetchHistory`. Two identical requests fire simultaneously.

**Fix**: Remove the explicit `await fetchHistory()` calls from `login`/`signup`/`logout` — the `useEffect` already handles it.

