## 🔵 Code Quality Issues

### 1. Data URLs stored in database as fallback

**File**: [generate/route.ts](file:///c:/Users/DELL/Documents/personal%20proejcts/image%20gen/iprintr/src/app/api/generate/route.ts#L150-L155)

When Vercel Blob upload fails, the fallback creates a `data:` URI:

```typescript
const base64 = imageBuffer.toString("base64");
imageUrl = `data:${mimeType};base64,${base64}`;
```

A typical generated image is 500KB–2MB. Base64 encoding inflates that by ~33%. Storing a ~2.6MB string in a Postgres `String` column is very expensive and will bloat your database rapidly. It also means the history API returns these massive strings on every page load.

> [!CAUTION]
> This can quickly make your database unusable. Consider failing gracefully instead of storing data URIs, or use a different storage fallback.

---

### 2. `any` types used extensively for error handling

**Files**: Multiple — [page.tsx:L36](file:///c:/Users/DELL/Documents/personal%20proejcts/image%20gen/iprintr/src/app/page.tsx#L36), [page.tsx:L81](file:///c:/Users/DELL/Documents/personal%20proejcts/image%20gen/iprintr/src/app/page.tsx#L81), [login/page.tsx:L27](file:///c:/Users/DELL/Documents/personal%20proejcts/image%20gen/iprintr/src/app/login/page.tsx#L27), all API routes

Every `catch` block uses `catch (err: any)`. TypeScript strict mode is enabled but `any` defeats the purpose. Use `unknown` and narrow with type guards:

```typescript
catch (err: unknown) {
  const message = err instanceof Error ? err.message : "Unknown error";
}
```

---

### 3. No user session persistence

The user is stored in React state only (`useState<User | null>`). Refreshing the page loses the login entirely. Consider using `localStorage`, cookies, or a proper session mechanism.

---

### 4. Auth route duplicated logic

**File**: [auth/route.ts](file:///c:/Users/DELL/Documents/personal%20proejcts/image%20gen/iprintr/src/app/api/auth/route.ts#L16-L47)

The `signup` and `login` branches are character-for-character identical (both do find-or-create). This should be a single code path.

---

### 5. `fetchWithRetry` is only used for Gemini, but Hugging Face could also benefit

**File**: [generate/route.ts](file:///c:/Users/DELL/Documents/personal%20proejcts/image%20gen/iprintr/src/app/api/generate/route.ts#L15-L35)

The custom retry logic with exponential backoff only wraps the Gemini REST call. The HuggingFace SDK call (which is also rate-limited per their free tier) doesn't get any retry logic. Either use the SDK's built-in retry or wrap it similarly.

